        "Romans 5:12 has a famous puzzle. Paul says death spread to all <em>eph’ hō</em> all sinned. Most modern translations read ‘because all sinned’. The Latin Bible Augustine used read <em>in quo</em>, ‘in whom’, meaning all sinned ‘in Adam’. Much Western teaching on original guilt grew from that reading. Eastern Christians tend to stress inherited death and corruption rather than inherited guilt. This is a real difference between traditions.",
        "Then Paul builds his ‘much more’ argument (5:15, 17, 20). Adam’s act brought condemnation; Christ’s act brings ‘justification of life’ (5:18). The grace is not just equal to the damage. It overflows it.",
        "Paul switches between ‘the many’ (5:15, 19) and ‘all’ (5:18). In Hebrew idiom ‘the many’ can mean everyone, as in Isaiah 53:11–12. Readers disagree on how wide Paul means the second ‘all’ to be. But the shape of the passage is clear: Christ’s reach is at least as wide as Adam’s."
