        "<em>Perichōrēsis</em> comes from <em>peri</em>, ‘around’, and <em>chōreō</em>, ‘to go, make room, contain’. It suggests going around, encompassing, mutual indwelling. Wikipedia’s summary of the scholarship is blunt: suggested links with Greek words for dancing ‘are not grounded in Greek etymology or early Christian use, but are modern in origin’.",
        "The history matters too. The verb appears in Gregory of Nazianzus, who used it of the two natures in Christ. The noun first appears in Maximus the Confessor in the seventh century. John of Damascus in the eighth century applied it fully to the Trinity, and it became the technical term. The Latin West later spoke of <em>circumincessio</em>.",
        "So ‘dance’ is a picture, not a translation. It is a good picture if we remember what it pictures: persons who make room for each other, live in each other and never lose themselves in each other."
