        "In the Latin West, Augustine had already said something similar in <em>On the Trinity</em> VI: ‘each are in each, and all in each, and each in all, and all in all, and all are one’. Hilary of Poitiers had written on the same theme from John 14:10 (<em>On the Trinity</em> 3.1).",
        "Notice the balance. ‘Without coalescing or mingling’ keeps the persons distinct. ‘In one another’ keeps them one. This guards against both tritheism (three gods) and a God who is only one person wearing three masks."
