        "Some theologians criticise modern ‘social’ views of the Trinity. Karen Kilby, for example, argued that writers often fill the idea of perichoresis with their own ideals of community and then project them back onto God. Reformed writers (such as Lane Tipton in the <em>Westminster Theological Journal</em>) stress that perichoresis belongs to God alone and must not blur the line between Creator and creature. They worry that ‘we are drawn into the dance’ can sound as if we become part of the Trinity.",
        "These are real warnings. A careful relational answer accepts them. We do not become a fourth member of the Trinity. We share in the Son’s relationship to the Father by grace, through his humanity and by the Spirit. John of Damascus’s phrase ‘without coalescing or mingling’ applies to our union with God as well.",
